# numbers-division.kb - Numbers / Division
# Format: concept | intent | answer | keywords | patterns | tl | bis
# 2 entries, one genuinely distinct fact per line.
divide by zero | definition | Dividing by zero is undefined rather than infinite, and the reason is worth being precise about because the two are constantly confused. Division is a request for the number that multiplied by the divisor gives the dividend, so asking what divided by zero is asks what multiplied by zero gives zero, and every number does that, which means there is no single answer to return. An infinity is not a number that can be produced this way, so the answer is not a value at all, it is the absence of one. This is different from a limit: a sequence of numbers divided by an ever-smaller positive number grows without bound, and that is a real, useful result, but at zero itself there is nothing to limit to. The other common confusion is 0 divided by 0, which is undefined for the same reason rather than as a special case, since zero is not a number you are allowed to divide by either. | undefined, infinity, zero, quotient, limit, NaN, divide | why is zero divided by zero undefined, what is division by zero, is 0 divided by 0 zero | zero | zero
